# Netlify: what the export puts in the published folder that is not part of the site answers the
# not-found page. `404!` rather than `404`: Netlify does not apply a plain rule to a path where a
# file exists, and these are files that are there. Netlify has no rule that serves a list of
# paths and refuses the rest, as the Apache and nginx configs do, so publish the folder the build
# writes and never a checkout. Cloudflare Pages takes no 404 rules from this file, so there
# nothing refuses .htaccess (it holds the same public headers as _headers, nothing secret), and
# GitHub Pages serves all three hosting files.
/_headers        /404.html  404!
/_redirects      /404.html  404!
/.htaccess       /404.html  404!
/metadata.json   /404.html  404!
